      <p>The most common audit finding is not a missing feature — it is an undocumented exclusion. Teams discover two tools have been suppressing different purchaser windows for months, which is why winback looks broken in one dashboard and fine in the other.</p>
      <p>Second finding: consent captured without source tags. When every popup writes "webform" to the same field, welcome branching is guesswork and migrating away from Activecampaign cannot be evaluated fairly, because neither tool receives the signal it needs.</p>
      <p>Third: app costs reviewed annually as a lump sum. Split fees by layer and by job; the number that shocks finance is usually the capture or proof app nobody has opened since onboarding.</p>
      <p>Fourth: sale-week behavior is the real benchmark. Tools that require a developer or a support ticket to pause a flow during BFCM cost more than their subscription suggests.</p>
